<crash-analysis>
The crash is a null pointer dereference in `vlc_object_typename`. The call chain is `vlm_New` -> `vlc_object_Log` -> `vlc_object_vaLog` -> `vlc_object_typename`.

The `vlm_New` function at `/src/vlc/src/input/vlm.c:137` calls `msg_Dbg( p_this, "creating VLM" );`. `p_this` is `VLC_OBJECT(libvlc)`, which is a cast of the `libvlc_int_t` pointer passed to `vlm_New`.

The `msg_Dbg` macro eventually calls `vlc_object_Log`. Inside `vlc_object_Log`, `vlc_object_vaLog` is called, which then calls `vlc_object_typename`.

The `vlc_object_typename` function is defined as `return vlc_internals(obj)->typename;`, where `vlc_internals(o)` is a macro for `((o)->priv)`. So the code is `return obj->priv->typename;`.

The crash occurs because `obj->priv` is `NULL`. In `vlm_New`, the `libvlc_int_t` object is not fully initialized when `msg_Dbg` is called. Specifically, the `priv` member of the `vlc_object_t` part of the `libvlc_int_t` object has not been allocated and initialized yet. This happens later in `vlc_object_init`.

The fix is to move the `msg_Dbg` call in `vlm_New` to after the `p_vlm` object has been successfully created and initialized by `vlc_custom_create`.

**Suggested Patch:**

```diff
--- a/src/input/vlm.c
+++ b/src/input/vlm.c
@@ -148,13 +148,13 @@
return p_vlm;
}

-    msg_Dbg( p_this, "creating VLM" );
-
p_vlm = vlc_custom_create( p_this, sizeof( *p_vlm ), "vlm daemon" );
if( !p_vlm )
{
vlc_mutex_unlock( &vlm_mutex );
return NULL;
}
+
+    msg_Dbg( p_this, "creating VLM" );

vlc_mutex_init( &p_vlm->lock );
vlc_mutex_init( &p_vlm->lock_manage );

```
</crash-analysis>

